# The Command Has Been Issued...!

## Language and Race in Ireland - Love of Language and Language Fanaticism - Hindi Fanaticism - Nehru's View

Younger brother!

"Let us suppose that tomorrow I am to depart from my homeland. If, at that moment, I am asked, 'What are you leaving behind for the people of the country? What is the final message you wish to give them?' I shall leave saying only this: if the Irish people wish to continue living as the Irish race, they must safeguard the Irish language."

De Valera spoke in this fashion: the hero who laboured arduously to retrieve his homeland, Ireland, from the British grip, and a man of ability worthy of the whole world's praise.

The British were immensely powerful. Their military strength inspired fear. They slaughtered those who resisted them. They crowed that even if an entire country were thrust into a pit of ruin, they would never relinquish their domination. They did not merely crow, but committed many cruelties without shame. When the people stood bewildered, asking how these rulers could be driven out and defeated and how the homeland could be liberated, de Valera told them: "People of the country, if you dare to die, the nation will live. If you wander about seeking only to remain alive, the nation will die." He infused them with courage, rallied them, conducted a war of liberation, and won. That great freedom fighter says that language must be protected if a race is not to perish.

Yes, is that not true? Nature has given each race a language of its own. That language establishes a "way of life" for its people. This is not formed in one or two years. It is fashioned little by little over many generations, accumulates, and assumes a great form. In the land where those people move, the forest where they hunt with delight, the rivers where they swim and play, the fields they plough for benefit, the workplaces where labour is joined with intellectual skill, and the homes where they mingle in happiness, they express their thoughts, listen to those of others, discuss them, form ideas, and discover ways to preserve and develop those ideas. The language fashioned for that people supports them in acquiring this excellent wealth. That is why scholars say language shapes a way of life.

A language is formed in accordance with climatic conditions.

Over many centuries, a particular population uses that language to express its thoughts and benefits by listening to it; ideas are thereby formed. That treasury is what the race has sought and acquired, and later gives to the world.

Knowing this, people say that language, race, and culture are interconnected.

Consciousness of language gives life to a people.

The distinct qualities and excellences belonging to a race were acquired through that language. If it loses the language, those qualities and excellences will fade and die; or they will mingle with other things, lose their form, and become fruitless. Those who studied and understood this well said that no race should allow its mother tongue to perish.

Ireland was in the British grip. Many evils surrounded it. Poverty poured down, famine raged, agriculture was destroyed, industry crushed, and commerce ruined.

Did the people of Ireland therefore ask, "We need food! We need work!" No. They asked, "We want our country! The government of the country must be in our hands!" For when a people remains in the grip of another country, it is not merely attacked by hunger, famine, and many forms of cruelty; its racial character is destroyed. Once that character has been destroyed, it forever loses even the courage to stand erect and demand its rights. It reaches a condition in which food for the stomach alone seems sufficient.

Younger brother, even if a bull is given abundant straw and grain, it yearns to break its tether and run freely. You have seen that, have you not? It does not remain content that its stomach is full and it has nothing more to worry about. It seems to say: "Someone has tied me up. I can move only as far as this rope permits. Why should I remain bound? Why should this man bind me and then give me straw and grain? Do I lack the strength to seek and obtain enough food for my own needs? How many green places there are! Do I not know how to run among them and feed with delight? But he has tied me!" The bull leaps, strains, lashes its tail, paws the ground, and, if one approaches, comes to gore and throw him aside.

But what do you see the same bull do when it has grown old? It eats whatever is placed before it and lies chewing the cud. It must repeatedly be prodded merely to make it stand. The vigour of its youth has died. It has concluded that this alone is henceforth its fate.

Those who impose domination believe that, if they can check the fierce rise of a people's desire for freedom by binding them or cutting them down, the enslaved people will later acquire the nature of an aged bull. They will eat what is given, do what they are told, and lie still.

Yet because "language" exists, no matter how long a ruler uses every weapon at his disposal to suppress an oppressed people, they do not entirely lose their sense of freedom. They may lack the courage to begin a war of liberation, but the feeling itself does not perish. Language is the reason.

Language enables them to express thoughts, say what is in their minds, ask whether this must forever be their fate, discuss whether their ancestors lived in this manner, and consider what those ancestors did to overcome distress when adversity came.

If it were enough merely to receive the thoughts arising from time to time, one could learn and speak any language. That would suffice. But momentary emotion alone is not enough to acquire a consciousness of freedom. People must know how their ancestors lived before enslavement, when they possessed rights and lived in a manner admired by the world; they must speak of it. Only then, as Bharathiyar said, does one reach the state in which "at the very mention of the land of our fathers, a power rises in our breath." Each people can attain that state only through its own mother tongue. The mother tongue must therefore be protected.

You may ask: "Anna, is so much explanation necessary? Who does not know this? Even a dull-witted man would not deny it. Is not language the eye of a race? No base son would think of losing it!"

I know you will joyfully accept de Valera's declaration that, if he had to give a final message, it would be: "Protect the Irish language so that Ireland may live." I know too, younger brother, that you will ask whether anyone could possibly deny the counsel of that able man. Yet in our own land there is a great personage who ridicules and opposes such impassioned counsel as de Valera's: protect your language, for only then will your country remain yours and remain in your possession. Do you ask, "Here?" Yes, younger brother, here itself, in this same Tamil Nadu where peerless literature was created two thousand years ago. Do you ask, "Now?" Yes, younger brother. I speak a painful truth. He exists now, when the feelings of language, race, country, and freedom blaze within your heart. That virtuous man goes about speaking and jeering that you and I, and the many hundreds of thousands of disciplined fighters who work beside us, are simpletons incapable of doing anything.

De Valera said, "Do not lose your language, for then even the path to freedom will be destroyed."

This man says: "What madness is this talk of 'my language' and 'the good language'? What is this argument about mother tongue and father tongue? What is this obstinacy of saying, 'I shall study this language and not that one'? This language fanaticism must not be allowed."

The world praises de Valera.

Those around him praise this great genius who disparages even his mother tongue!

You ask angrily, younger brother, "In what asylum did you find such a lifeless creature?" But must I not answer in sorrow? The man who wanders about speaking such degrading words occupies the highest office. Kamaraj, younger brother, the Chief Minister! It is he who says we suffer from language fanaticism, that we wander about shouting "Tamil! Tamil!" and that it makes no difference which language it is.

In Ireland, de Valera says that a life of rights will endure if the language is protected.

In Tamil Nadu, where an army stands rallied to protect its language, Kamaraj governs while saying that the path of language must not be followed.

We have endured countless sufferings. We lost sovereignty and forts; we lost commerce and stand deprived of many honours; we lost industrial wealth and comrades; we lost ships and the ability to compose epics. We endured all these afflictions. But must we endure even this cruelty: that in Tamil Nadu, during the days when we are alive, a Tamil governing Tamils mocks as "language fanaticism" the attachment a Tamil must show to Tamil, the valour that rises to fight when the mother tongue is gravely endangered, and the courage that resists degrading the mother tongue and introducing an alien, demonic language? Must we stand listening to it?

Kamaraj says: "Hey! Hey! If you do not endure it, what will you do, leap and jump?" In Tamil Nadu, where epic Tamil once flourished, this stale Tamil now gives off its odour. I call it stale, younger brother, because Kamaraj's astonishing argument that there must be no language fanaticism is old merchandise we have heard again and again from others until it has turned sour. Mere stale brew!

Who gave Kamaraj the authority to decide what is attachment and what is fanaticism? People voted to seat him in high office, but did they do so to let him deliver the "final verdict" on language? Learned scholars, experts, and great poets say that much research remains to be conducted into virgin Tamil. This man has learned to attend to elections by searching for and securing the Padayachi of Kanur, the Reddiar of Vanur, the Mudaliar of Menur, the Marakkayar of Pallapatti, the Gounder of Papparapatti, and the men of Vadipatti and Vathalakundu. What opportunity has he had to understand the antiquity and refinement of Tamil, and its present condition? If he says four or five poets run to him when he calls, what answer can be given except that they seek him because he governs the country, not because he possesses the ability to govern letters?

It is not easy to frame definitions for attachment and fanaticism, nor is it possible to measure the two conditions.

He dislikes our girding ourselves to stand for the language, and therefore calls it fanaticism. That is all. Is he an expert who has discovered some unique method of classifying, testing, and weighing emotions? No one would say so; even he would be ashamed to claim it. Yet because he occupies the position of Chief Minister, he has an opportunity to say anything, and therefore speaks dogmatically. If a poor man goes to a gentleman's house to seek a loan and the gentleman's mad son leaps at him and bites his leg or cheek, what can the poor man do? He consoles himself that he has paid the interest in advance.

We are forced to endure whatever a ruler says. When we sit in a park, a bird may soil our shirt with its droppings. What can we do? We rise and seek another place. Does the bird deserve the title of supreme warrior merely because its droppings made us move elsewhere?

As though saying, "I came to rule the country, not to rule my tongue; it will do whatever it pleases," he speaks all manner of things.

He says, "I built schools." The late Raja Sir Annamalai Chettiar never once said, "I established a university." The government receives eighty crore rupees, yet Kamaraj says he built schools as though he were a philanthropist spending the vast fortune earned by his family eight generations ago. Listeners think, "Very well, let him speak from some peculiar temptation, enjoy his petty pleasure, and go." But must he go beyond that and offer a "commentary" on language too?

What is seen in Tamil Nadu today is not language fanaticism. Capable Tamils have engaged in the task of protecting the language from fanatics.

If asked for his final message, de Valera could have said countless things: "People of Ireland, remain united! Remain strong! Labour and seek wealth! Share what you earn with others! Do not forget the wealth of education! Do not forget morality! Worship the Lord!" Instead, he said, "Protect the Irish language. Only then will the Irish nation endure." Fortunately, no Kamaraj arose in his country to preach against language fanaticism. But what would a British autocrat intent on dominating Ireland say on hearing de Valera? Surely: "Shame! How has de Valera lost his senses like this? He is stirring up language fanaticism!"

Does that mean Kamaraj is an autocrat seeking to enslave Tamils? No, no. He has not undertaken such arduous work. He is content merely to serve as a helper to the northern Hindi autocrats who plan to enslave Tamils by introducing Hindi. Yet he lectures us that there must be no fanaticism.

But what is actually taking place?

Those possessed by the fanaticism of domination have declared Hindi the official language of all India.

The decision about language was made at a moment overflowing with joy at release from the grip of British imperialism and eagerness, immediately upon freedom, to organise procedures and show the world an ability to frame a constitution quickly. They resolved that Hindi should be the official language.

Even then, indignation and differences of opinion arose. But the festive mood curtailed debate. Many could not clearly grasp the scale and nature of the danger. The dominant thought was: "The white man has departed. We ourselves are now Viceroy and Governor; the ministerial posts belong to us." That delight left no room to think of anything else. The advocates of Hindi domination accomplished their purpose.

Even then, however, only the Dravidian movement in Tamil Nadu remained vigilant, pointing out and warning of the approaching danger.

Before "independence" was obtained, Congressmen had already tasted office. In those days, Acharya made Hindi a compulsory subject; fierce opposition arose; a thousand went to prison; and two young bulls died there. These events occupy a place in the heroic annals of Tamil Nadu.

In those days, anti-Hindi resistance was described as the handiwork of disappointed politicians. Acharya sought to explain the situation in precisely those terms. Today, he himself has become an opponent of Hindi.

Younger brother, I do not know whether you heard this fifteen or twenty years ago: today they call Hindi the official language, but then they emphatically called it the national language.

After we explained why neither Hindi nor any other language could be the national language of the Tamil, conducted intense propaganda and agitations, went to prison, and endured many hardships, the arrogance of Hindi supporters diminished. Today they speak with considerable restraint: "India has fourteen national languages and Hindi is one of them. But it is spoken by the largest number, and is therefore the common and official language."

They have not abandoned their aim of Hindi domination; they have merely changed their method and their speech.

Except for Tamils, others showed little concern or responsibility regarding the relationship among language, race, and culture. Here, the fire of enthusiasm never went out. Acharya tried and failed. He thundered, "Let us see who governs!" He made the false calculation: "We are the ones who opposed the white man. These people arguing for language are incapable of fighting and cannot endure repression." He ordered baton charges. Heads broke, but minds did not. He was bewildered. When compulsory Hindi was abolished, Tamils sang and danced in delight: "Hindi maiden who came running, listen! This is not the land you sought!"

Because Kamaraj does not know that history, he thunders today: "An anti-Hindi agitation? Let them conduct it; we shall see!" He threatens us that guns and bullets are available.

A hand stained with blood, a heart without compassion, a nature that refuses enlightenment, and intoxication with authority: we know Kamaraj's party is filled with all these. Knowing it, our General Council at Kumarapalayam has resolved to oppose Hindi.

Beloved younger brother who waited for the command! The command has been issued. At the end of August, there will be an opportunity and a feast for your strength!
